1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,749
Have a look at this picture. It was printed
in a London newspaper in March 1879.

2
00:00:06,416 --> 00:00:08,872
Two British officers are
riding for their lives.

3
00:00:08,912 --> 00:00:11,112
One is clutching his regiment's colours.

4
00:00:11,836 --> 00:00:15,209
The artist wasn't there. But
the battle behind it was real.

5
00:00:15,968 --> 00:00:20,453
On 22 January 1879, a Zulu
army destroyed a British

6
00:00:20,493 --> 00:00:24,658
camp here, beneath a
hill called Isandlwana.

7
00:00:25,570 --> 00:00:29,906
More than 1,300 men on the British
side were killed in a single afternoon.

8
00:00:30,735 --> 00:00:34,027
The usual story is about what
went wrong for the British.

9
00:00:34,786 --> 00:00:39,378
But that's only half of it. The real
question is how the Zulu army did it.

10
00:00:42,537 --> 00:00:45,004
And the answer starts
with the army itself.

11
00:00:45,798 --> 00:00:48,591
This is the story of Isandlwana.

12
00:00:52,341 --> 00:00:55,152
In December 1878, Britain's
High Commissioner,

13
00:00:55,192 --> 00:00:58,535
Sir Bartle Frere, sent the
Zulu king an ultimatum.

14
00:00:59,236 --> 00:01:01,808
The king was Cetshwayo.
He was told, among other

15
00:01:01,848 --> 00:01:04,293
things, to break up the
Zulu military system.

16
00:01:04,912 --> 00:01:06,544
No king could agree to that.

17
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:10,456
London hadn't approved a
war. Frere went ahead anyway.

18
00:01:11,041 --> 00:01:13,926
In January, British columns
crossed into Zululand.

19
00:01:13,966 --> 00:01:16,921
One, under Lord Chelmsford,
crossed at Rorke's Drift.

20
00:01:18,531 --> 00:01:21,150
Now, to understand what happened
next, you need to understand

21
00:01:21,190 --> 00:01:23,309
the army Chelmsford was walking towards.

22
00:01:24,184 --> 00:01:26,768
Zulu men were organised
into regiments by age,

23
00:01:26,808 --> 00:01:29,937
called amabutho. When the
king called, they came.

24
00:01:30,661 --> 00:01:33,547
Most fought with a short stabbing
spear and a cowhide shield.

25
00:01:33,587 --> 00:01:36,937
Many carried guns too, but mostly
old ones, with poor powder.

26
00:01:37,638 --> 00:01:41,696
What they had was speed, discipline,
and a plan of attack every warrior knew.

27
00:01:42,397 --> 00:01:44,446
They called it the horns of the buffalo.

28
00:01:45,147 --> 00:01:48,370
The chest pins the enemy in front.
The horns run wide and close

29
00:01:48,410 --> 00:01:51,353
round the sides. The loins
wait behind, in reserve.

30
00:01:54,127 --> 00:01:57,324
In the middle of January, the
army set out from the king's

31
00:01:57,364 --> 00:02:00,797
homestead. Estimates run from
about 20,000 to 25,000 men.

32
00:02:01,591 --> 00:02:04,442
It was led by an experienced
commander, Ntshingwayo

33
00:02:04,482 --> 00:02:07,692
kaMahole, with Mavumengwana kaNdlela.

34
00:02:09,243 --> 00:02:12,268
Chelmsford pitched his camp
at the foot of Isandlwana.

35
00:02:12,934 --> 00:02:16,748
He was expecting to find the Zulu
army. He just didn't know where it was.

36
00:02:17,333 --> 00:02:20,474
The Zulu commanders moved their
regiments across country in small

37
00:02:20,514 --> 00:02:24,212
groups, and brought them together
in a valley called Ngwebeni.

38
00:02:24,936 --> 00:02:28,193
It was just a few miles from the
British camp. Tens of thousands

39
00:02:28,233 --> 00:02:31,061
of men. And nobody in the
camp knew they were there.

40
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:34,655
Before dawn on the 22nd,
Chelmsford got reports

41
00:02:34,695 --> 00:02:36,981
of Zulu forces to the south-east.

42
00:02:37,705 --> 00:02:41,032
He took a large part of his force
out of camp to go after them.

43
00:02:41,791 --> 00:02:45,924
About 1,800 men stayed behind, under
Lieutenant-Colonel Henry Pulleine.

44
00:02:45,964 --> 00:02:49,924
Mid-morning, Colonel Anthony
Durnford arrived with more troops.

45
00:02:50,590 --> 00:02:53,743
Several Zulu accounts say they
weren't planning to fight that day,

46
00:02:53,783 --> 00:02:57,910
because of the moon. Historians still
debate how far the attack was planned.

47
00:02:59,461 --> 00:03:02,730
Around midday, a patrol of
British horsemen rode up onto the

48
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:05,597
high ground and looked down
into the Ngwebeni valley.

49
00:03:06,321 --> 00:03:08,290
The whole Zulu army was there.

50
00:03:09,921 --> 00:03:13,073
The regiments didn't wait for
orders. They rose and came on.

51
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:16,056
Over the hills, across a
front of several miles,

52
00:03:16,096 --> 00:03:18,250
the horns of the buffalo began to open.

53
00:03:21,691 --> 00:03:25,517
In front of the camp, the British
infantry formed a long firing line.

54
00:03:26,241 --> 00:03:29,394
For a while, it held. Rifle
and artillery fire stopped

55
00:03:29,434 --> 00:03:32,250
the chest at a distance.
Warriors went to ground.

56
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:37,601
Then, by Zulu accounts, an
induna named Mkhosana kaMvundlana

57
00:03:37,641 --> 00:03:40,492
stood up in the open and
shamed his men for lying down.

58
00:03:41,193 --> 00:03:44,833
He was shot dead. But the regiment
rose, and the charge went in.

59
00:03:46,308 --> 00:03:49,507
Meanwhile the left horn was
working round one side of the camp.

60
00:03:50,126 --> 00:03:54,068
And the right horn had gone wide,
behind the hill itself, out of sight.

61
00:03:56,647 --> 00:03:58,755
It came down onto the track
back to Rorke's Drift,

62
00:03:58,795 --> 00:04:00,926
the camp's road home, and closed it.

63
00:04:03,695 --> 00:04:07,312
The British line began to pull
back. And as it did, it broke.

64
00:04:08,168 --> 00:04:11,541
In the early afternoon, the camp
was overrun. The fighting went

65
00:04:11,581 --> 00:04:14,676
on, through the tents and
wagons, and along every path out.

66
00:04:15,377 --> 00:04:18,727
That afternoon a partial eclipse
dimmed the sun, but by then,

67
00:04:18,767 --> 00:04:21,432
the battle for the camp
had already been decided.

68
00:04:22,982 --> 00:04:26,325
More than 1,300 men on the British
side were killed. Several hundred

69
00:04:26,365 --> 00:04:29,409
of them were African soldiers,
fighting for the British.

70
00:04:30,269 --> 00:04:33,779
The Zulu army paid heavily
too. Nobody made a full count.

71
00:04:33,819 --> 00:04:37,496
Estimates run from about
1,000 to more than 3,000 dead.

72
00:04:38,290 --> 00:04:41,837
Most of what we know comes from British
records. Zulu accounts were often

73
00:04:41,877 --> 00:04:45,332
collected later, through
interpreters, by British interviewers.

74
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:50,311
So some of this story we only hear
second-hand. But the outcome isn't in doubt.

75
00:04:51,861 --> 00:04:56,570
That same afternoon and night, part of the
reserve attacked the small post at Rorke's

76
00:04:56,610 --> 00:05:00,946
Drift, by most accounts against the
king's wishes. About 150 men held it.

77
00:05:01,566 --> 00:05:04,927
And Britain did what an empire
could do. It sent more men.

78
00:05:05,802 --> 00:05:09,497
On 4 July 1879, at
Ulundi, the Zulu army was

79
00:05:09,537 --> 00:05:13,273
defeated, and the royal homestead burned.

80
00:05:14,149 --> 00:05:17,488
In August, Cetshwayo was
captured and sent into

81
00:05:17,528 --> 00:05:21,086
exile. Zululand was
divided into 13 chiefdoms.

82
00:05:21,764 --> 00:05:25,218
So the Zulu won the battle.
But the kingdom lost the war.

83
00:05:27,968 --> 00:05:32,070
In January 1999, a memorial was
unveiled on the battlefield.

84
00:05:32,110 --> 00:05:35,347
It is a bronze isiqu,
a necklace of valour.

85
00:05:36,024 --> 00:05:39,235
So, how did the Zulu
army win at Isandlwana?

86
00:05:40,110 --> 00:05:43,460
Not by luck. And not just
because of British mistakes.

87
00:05:44,289 --> 00:05:47,383
It won because a nation could
put a trained army in the field,

88
00:05:47,423 --> 00:05:51,005
move it unseen, and attack in
a way every man understood.

89
00:05:51,648 --> 00:05:54,302
Because its commanders used
the ground. And because,

90
00:05:54,342 --> 00:05:57,553
when the charge stalled, its
regiments got up and went on.

91
00:05:58,173 --> 00:06:00,280
And it won because the
British camp it found was

92
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:03,183
split, spread out, and too sure of itself.

93
00:06:05,169 --> 00:06:07,648
This has been OrigineX Human Archives.

94
00:06:08,314 --> 00:06:11,919
If you think this record is
worth keeping, please subscribe.
